<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristobulus I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 104–103 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristobulus I was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> he was the first of the Hasmoneans to take the title of king alongside the high priesthood. During his reign of about a year he imprisoned his mother and most of his brothers and extended Judaean rule over the Itureans in the north, forcing them to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristobulus-i.jpg" alt="Aristobulus I" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristobulus_I_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aristobulus I was the eldest of the five sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> name was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, and Aristobulus, a Greek name meaning best-advising, was an epithet he was the first Hasmonean to adopt. He was also called Philhellene, an admirer of Greek culture, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> does not explain why. With his brother Antigonus he was entrusted by their father with the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>. The brothers besieged the city and defeated a relief army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> led by Antiochus IX Cyzicenus. They captured and razed Samaria near the end of John's reign and enslaved its inhabitants, then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beit-shean">Scythopolis</a> and the whole region south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> had left the country to his wife, with Aristobulus to receive only the high priesthood. Instead Aristobulus had his mother imprisoned, where she starved to death, and so secured both the high priesthood and the kingship. He imprisoned all his brothers except Antigonus. Josephus states that he was the first Jew to establish a monarchy since the return from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a> were not troubled by his taking the title of king, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a> opposed it strongly, since they held that kingship belonged to the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>.</p><p>He made war on the Ituraeans and took territory from them, compelling them to accept Jewish law and circumcision in order to stay on their land. Most scholars place their territory in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, though its exact extent is unknown. Kenneth Atkinson finds no evidence of forced Judaization in Galilee during his reign. Aristobulus's coins carry the title of high priest and not that of king, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, with the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. Atkinson holds that the absence of a royal title does not show he was never a monarch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Aristobulus fell ill with sudden abdominal pains and withdrew to his palace during the festival of Sukkot. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, rumours spread by his wife and conspirators told him that Antigonus, who came to the Temple in armour, meant to seize the throne. The queen is said to have bribed the messenger so that Antigonus came armed to his brother, and Aristobulus killed him. He then grew rapidly worse and died soon after, in 103 BCE. The queen released his brothers and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jannaeus">Alexander Jannaeus</a> as the next king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aristobulus I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hasmonean rulers combined the high priesthood with political power, and from <b>Aristobulus I</b> took the title king.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
